I love virtual reality and I also love  Grand Theft Auto I started this feature  with the full intention of gushing about  how excited I was to combine them and  then continuing on to explain how great  the experience truly was once I got it  working and you would think that the  marriage of the two would yield some  sort of glorious world simulator  permeated with vertiginous freedom and  lifelike immersion well I regret to  inform you that that's not quite the  case let me start by saying that a I  typically have a high tolerance for VR  sickness and B I spent a long time  modifying the field of view options to  try and make GTA 5 work well with the  oculus rift but after several attempts  at extended play all of which ended with  me on the verge of throwing up laying on  my desk nursing a cup of tea I've  decided that I'm pretty done trying to  force games to be VR ready when they're  very clearly not that's not to say there  isn't potential here activities like  driving around can be immersive and  downright blissful with the riff  strapped to your face and it's amazing  to be able to Crane your neck and look  out the back window or the terrified  passenger in your stolen vehicle but the  camera too frequently gets out of sync  with the direction you're facing leaving  you turning strange angles just to look  straight until you recenter the rip in  addition to this the common issue or VR  head look and joystick look conflict is  more of a problem than usual here  turning with the joystick results in a  sickening 90-degree whip pan when you  try to turn your head at the same time  and turning using only your head is  novel for a while until your neck begins  to cedar with the pain of being used as  a steering wheel and your stomach turns  because you've been rapidly swaying your  head back and forth for 20 damn minutes  further when the game takes over camera  control such as when entering a vehicle  or being run over by one the nausea  increases by about a facto